1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,012
The Water Cycle.

2
00:00:01,962 --> 00:00:12,162
Earth's water cycle moves approximately 1,386 million cubic kilometers of water through the hydrosphere continuously, driven by solar energy.

3
00:00:12,162 --> 00:00:26,537
Oceans contain 96.5 percent of this total; freshwater in glaciers, ice caps, and groundwater accounts for the remaining 3.5 percent, with only 0.3 percent readily accessible in lakes and rivers.

4
00:00:26,537 --> 00:00:39,475
Evaporation from the ocean surface transfers roughly 434,000 cubic kilometers of water vapor into the atmosphere annually — an average rate of 1 meter of water loss per year from the ocean surface.

5
00:00:39,475 --> 00:00:45,325
Transpiration from vegetation adds another 71,000 cubic kilometers per year.

6
00:00:45,325 --> 00:00:58,225
Atmospheric water vapor content at any given moment totals approximately 13,000 cubic kilometers, equivalent to about 25 millimeters of precipitation globally if all condensed simultaneously.

7
00:00:58,225 --> 00:01:10,050
Global precipitation totals roughly 498,000 cubic kilometers per year, of which 385,000 falls over oceans and 113,000 over land.

8
00:01:10,050 --> 00:01:29,600
Residence times vary dramatically across reservoirs: the average water molecule remains in the atmosphere for 9 days, in rivers for 2 to 6 weeks, in natural lakes for 50 to 100 years, in groundwater aquifers for hundreds to tens of thousands of years, and in glaciers for 20 to 100 years.

9
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:36,475
Water in deep ice sheets, such as those in Antarctica, can remain locked for up to 800,000 years.

10
00:01:36,475 --> 00:01:48,925
The hydrologic cycle maintains an approximately steady state over human timescales: precipitation minus evaporation over land equals runoff to the ocean, balancing net evaporation over oceans.

11
00:01:48,925 --> 00:01:53,975
This closed-loop system has operated for at least 3.8 billion years.

